title: Work-up of mantle cell lymphoma
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''NCCN approach:'' Establish the diagnosis and disease extent in [[Mantle cell lymphoma]]; distinguish essential assessments from tests indicated by presentation. Recommendations below are category 2A.
* ''Essential diagnostic testing:'' Adequate immunophenotyping, including cyclin D1, SOX11, and Ki-67; ''TP53 sequencing''. Document blastoid/pleomorphic morphology. FISH for t(11;14) helps resolve diagnostic uncertainty; consider CCND2/CCND3 rearrangements in cyclin D1-negative cases. p53 immunohistochemistry is a frontline surrogate requiring sequencing confirmation.
* ''Essential clinical/laboratory assessment:'' Examine nodes, Waldeyer’s ring, liver, and spleen; record performance status and B symptoms. Obtain CBC/differential, comprehensive metabolic panel, LDH, and hepatitis B screening (HBsAg and anti-HBc; further virologic evaluation if positive).
* ''Imaging/pretreatment:'' If therapy is planned, obtain ''PET/CT (preferred)'' or diagnostic contrast CT of chest/abdomen/pelvis. Obtain echocardiogram or MUGA for an anthracycline-containing regimen; pregnancy testing when applicable before therapy.
* ''GI staging:'' Endoscopy/colonoscopy is ''essential to confirm stage I–II disease'', because occult involvement is common. Otherwise use selectively, including for GI symptoms; routine endoscopy in advanced disease rarely changes management.
* ''Other selected tests:'' Marrow biopsy ± aspirate for apparent localized disease or unexplained cytopenias; contrast neck CT, beta-2-microglobulin, and hepatitis C testing as indicated. Check uric acid/assess tumor lysis with high burden or elevated LDH. Perform lumbar puncture for blastoid disease or central nervous system symptoms. Discuss fertility preservation when relevant.

Sources: NCCN B-Cell Lymphomas v4.2026 (May 20, 2026), MANT-1 and footnotes; Discussion MS-84–86.

